{
  "dom_id": "step-q121713",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14232470/assets/ecb02db9984510219c6209392c9f35df52eca11dacc802a133aa51c4e5065ede.png)\n\nA drone $D$ takes off from a point $P$ and climbs with an angle of elevation of ${60}^{∘}$ and a vertical speed of $4\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$ Assuming the angle of elevation doesn't change, calculate the actual speed of the drone in the direction it is traveling.\nRound your answer to two decimal places.",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14232470/assets/69314419f6e0b93236fc048c6b9b6b262b09ff2cc061996353fbe23870a12726.png)\n\nLet $y$ denote the vertical distance from the drone's projection to the starting point, and $z$ be the distance between the drone and the starting point, as shown above.\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $y$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ={60}^{∘},$ the sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({60}^{∘}) & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ z & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(z) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=4\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\cdot 4≈4.62\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the speed of the drone is approximately $4.62\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$",
